reasoning_over_luck.evidence:
Chance sources: the card deal decides how distinctive the murderer's cards are, and the role deal decides seats. Results depend on:
- the Scientist encoding 8 semantic hints (16 options per round) toward one of 4 own combos;
- investigators inferring the seat and combo from hints and from talk in which the Murderer lies;
- the Witness balancing helping against revealing themselves;
- the Murderer reading talk and accusation patterns to pick the hunt target.

Under all-good play the case is usually solved. The result then hinges on the hunt, a player choice informed by who signalled and who accused what, not a random draw. A blind hunt sits at 25-33%, and a perceptive Murderer beats that. A careful Witness lowers it. Chance from the deal is maybe 20-30% of variance and can be overcome by clear hints. A reasoning player is clearly favoured.
